---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import DeepDiveLinks from '../../components/DeepDiveLinks.astro';
import DownsidesSection from '../../components/DownsidesSection.astro';
---

<GuideLayout
  title="1 — Switch Your Browser"
  description="The easiest first step. 5 minutes to escape Google's tracking."
  level={1}
  nextGuide={{ href: "/guides/linux", label: "2 — Install Linux" }}
>

  <h2>Why Your Browser Matters</h2>
  <div class="content-block">
    <p>
      Your browser is the single most important piece of software on your computer. Every website you visit,
      every search you make, every form you fill out — it all goes through your browser. If you're using
      Google Chrome, congratulations: you're running a program made by an advertising company whose entire
      business model is knowing everything about you.
    </p>
    <p>
      Chrome isn't free because Google is generous. Chrome is free because <strong>you are the product</strong>.
      Every tab you open feeds Google's profile of you. Your browsing history, your bookmarks, your passwords,
      your autofill data — all of it flows back to Google's servers where it's used to sell ads targeted at you.
    </p>
    <p>
      The good news? Switching your browser is the single easiest thing on this entire site. Five minutes
      and you've already built the first section of your tech fence.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="flowing-line"></div>

  <h2>Pick Your New Browser</h2>
  <div class="content-block">
    <p>You have four solid options. Pick one and move on — don't overthink it.</p>
    <p>
      <strong>A note on Mozilla:</strong> Mozilla, the organization behind Firefox, receives a significant
      portion of its revenue from Google (~$500M/year) through a default search engine deal. This is a valid
      concern. However, Firefox is still open-source, still the most privacy-respecting mainstream browser,
      and still the only major browser engine not controlled by Google. The alternative is letting Google's
      Chromium monopolize the web entirely. Use Firefox — but stay aware.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="browser-grid">
    <div class="browser-card">
      <img src="/images/firefox-logo.webp" alt="" class="browser-card-bg" width="120" height="120" />
      <h3>Firefox — The Balanced Choice</h3>
      <p>
        Mozilla Firefox is the most popular privacy-focused browser. It's open-source, has a massive extension
        library, and is what PewDiePie uses alongside LibreWolf. Works on Windows, macOS, Linux, Android, and
        iOS. Syncs across devices with a Firefox account (encrypted, not sold to advertisers). For most people,
        this is the right answer.
      </p>
      <p><a href="https://www.mozilla.org/firefox/new/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Download Firefox →</a></p>
    </div>

    <div class="browser-card">
      <img src="/images/LibreWolf_icon.webp" alt="" class="browser-card-bg" width="120" height="120" />
      <h3>LibreWolf — Firefox But Stricter</h3>
      <p>
        A fork of Firefox with all telemetry removed and privacy settings cranked to maximum out of the box.
        No Mozilla telemetry, no Pocket, no sponsored shortcuts. Firefox for people who don't want to spend
        20 minutes tweaking about:config. Downside: no auto-update, no Firefox Sync by default.
      </p>
      <p><a href="https://librewolf.net/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Download LibreWolf →</a></p>
    </div>

    <div class="browser-card">
      <img src="/images/Brave_lion_icon.webp" alt="" class="browser-card-bg" width="120" height="120" />
      <h3>Brave — The Easy Switch from Chrome</h3>
      <p>
        Built on Chromium (same engine as Chrome) so all your Chrome extensions work. Built-in ad and tracker
        blocker. Familiar interface for Chrome users. Controversy: has its own crypto token (BAT) and opt-in
        ads. Privacy purists don't fully trust it, but it's still a massive upgrade from Chrome.
      </p>
      <p><a href="https://brave.com/download/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Download Brave →</a></p>
    </div>

    <div class="browser-card">
      <img src="/images/Mullvad_Browser_logo.webp" alt="" class="browser-card-bg" width="120" height="120" />
      <h3>Mullvad Browser — The Cybersecurity Expert's Choice</h3>
      <p>
        Built by the Tor Project and Mullvad VPN. Designed to minimize browser fingerprinting — every
        Mullvad Browser user looks identical to websites. Comes with uBlock Origin pre-installed. No sync,
        no accounts, no frills. For those who want maximum anti-fingerprinting without the Tor network.
      </p>
      <p><a href="https://mullvad.net/en/browser" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Download Mullvad Browser →</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="flowing-line"></div>

  <h2>Step-by-Step Setup</h2>

  <div class="step"="1">
    <span class="step-number">Step 1</span>
    <h3>Install Your New Browser</h3>
    <p>
      Download the installer from the official site of your chosen browser:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="https://www.mozilla.org/firefox/new/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Firefox</a> — mozilla.org</li>
      <li><a href="https://librewolf.net/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LibreWolf</a> — librewolf.net</li>
      <li><a href="https://brave.com/download/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Brave</a> — brave.com</li>
      <li><a href="https://mullvad.net/en/browser" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mullvad Browser</a> — mullvad.net</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Run the installer. Don't uninstall Chrome yet — keep it as a fallback for the first week while you adjust.
    </p>
    <label class="step-progress">
      <input type="checkbox" class="step-progress-checkbox" data-step-checkbox data-step-index="1" />
      <span class="step-progress-switch">
        <svg class="step-progress-check" width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 14 14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M3 7l3 3 5-5" /></svg>
      </span>
      <span class="step-progress-label">Mark step as done</span>
    </label>
  </div>

  <div class="step"="2">
    <span class="step-number">Step 2</span>
    <h3>Import Your Data</h3>
    <p>
      When you first launch your new browser, it will offer to import bookmarks, passwords, and history
      from Chrome. Say yes. This takes about 30 seconds and means you're not starting from scratch.
      In Firefox, go to <strong>Settings &rarr; Import Data</strong> if you missed the prompt.
    </p>
    <label class="step-progress">
      <input type="checkbox" class="step-progress-checkbox" data-step-checkbox data-step-index="2" />
      <span class="step-progress-switch">
        <svg class="step-progress-check" width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 14 14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M3 7l3 3 5-5" /></svg>
      </span>
      <span class="step-progress-label">Mark step as done</span>
    </label>
  </div>

  <div class="step"="3">
    <span class="step-number">Step 3</span>
    <h3>Change Your Default Search Engine</h3>
    <p>
      Go to your browser settings and change the default search engine from Google to
      <strong>DuckDuckGo</strong>. In Firefox: <strong>Settings &rarr; Search &rarr; Default Search Engine
      &rarr; DuckDuckGo</strong>. DuckDuckGo doesn't track your searches, doesn't build a profile on you,
      and doesn't filter results based on what it thinks you want to see.
    </p>
    <p>
      If DuckDuckGo's results aren't good enough for a specific query, you can always prefix your search
      with <strong>!g</strong> to get Google results through DuckDuckGo (though Google will still see that
      search). Other bangs: <strong>!w</strong> for Wikipedia, <strong>!yt</strong> for YouTube,
      <strong>!r</strong> for Reddit.
    </p>
    <label class="step-progress">
      <input type="checkbox" class="step-progress-checkbox" data-step-checkbox data-step-index="3" />
      <span class="step-progress-switch">
        <svg class="step-progress-check" width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 14 14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M3 7l3 3 5-5" /></svg>
      </span>
      <span class="step-progress-label">Mark step as done</span>
    </label>
  </div>

  <div class="step"="4">
    <span class="step-number">Step 4</span>
    <h3>Install uBlock Origin</h3>
    <p>
      This is non-negotiable. uBlock Origin is the best ad and tracker blocker available. Free,
      open-source, and blocks ads, trackers, malware domains, and annoyances. <strong>Not</strong> "uBlock" —
      that's a different, inferior extension.
    </p>
    <p>Install uBlock Origin for your browser:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Firefox / LibreWolf:</strong>
        <a href="https://addons.mozilla.org/en-US/firefox/addon/ublock-origin/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Firefox Add-ons Store</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Brave:</strong>
        <a href="https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/ublock-origin/cjpalhdlnbpafiamejdnhcphjbkeiagm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chrome Web Store</a>
        (Brave uses Chrome extensions — its built-in blocker exists but uBlock Origin is better)
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Mullvad Browser:</strong> Comes pre-installed. You're good.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>Important note:</strong> Google is actively killing ad blockers in Chrome through Manifest V3.
      This is one of the biggest reasons to leave Chrome — uBlock Origin will eventually stop working
      properly there. Firefox has committed to supporting Manifest V2 extensions indefinitely.
    </p>
    <label class="step-progress">
      <input type="checkbox" class="step-progress-checkbox" data-step-checkbox data-step-index="4" />
      <span class="step-progress-switch">
        <svg class="step-progress-check" width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 14 14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M3 7l3 3 5-5" /></svg>
      </span>
      <span class="step-progress-label">Mark step as done</span>
    </label>
  </div>

  <div class="step"="5">
    <span class="step-number">Step 5</span>
    <h3>Install Unhook for YouTube</h3>
    <p>
      This is the extension that PewDiePie highlighted in his "I Fixed YouTube" video. Unhook lets you
      selectively remove parts of the YouTube interface: the homepage feed, the recommended sidebar,
      Shorts, trending, comments — whatever you want. The idea is to turn YouTube from a slot machine
      into a tool you use intentionally.
    </p>
    <p>Install Unhook for your browser:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Firefox / LibreWolf:</strong>
        <a href="https://addons.mozilla.org/en-US/firefox/addon/youtube-recommended-videos/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Firefox Add-ons Store</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Brave:</strong>
        <a href="https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/unhook-remove-youtube-rec/khncfooichmfjbepaaaebmommgaepoid" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chrome Web Store</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Mullvad Browser:</strong>
        <a href="https://addons.mozilla.org/en-US/firefox/addon/youtube-recommended-videos/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Firefox Add-ons Store</a> (Mullvad is Firefox-based)
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      After installing, open the extension settings and disable: Homepage Feed, Recommended Videos,
      Shorts, and Trending. You can still search for videos and visit channels directly — you just won't
      be ambushed by the algorithm anymore.
    </p>
    <label class="step-progress">
      <input type="checkbox" class="step-progress-checkbox" data-step-checkbox data-step-index="5" />
      <span class="step-progress-switch">
        <svg class="step-progress-check" width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 14 14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M3 7l3 3 5-5" /></svg>
      </span>
      <span class="step-progress-label">Mark step as done</span>
    </label>
  </div>

  <div class="step"="6">
    <span class="step-number">Step 6</span>
    <h3>Consider Extra Privacy Extensions</h3>
    <p>These are optional but worth considering:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Privacy Badger</strong> (by the EFF) — Learns to block invisible trackers as you browse.
        Works alongside uBlock Origin without conflicts.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>HTTPS Everywhere</strong> — Forces encrypted connections where possible. Note: Firefox
        now has this built-in (Settings &rarr; Privacy &rarr; HTTPS-Only Mode), so you may not need the
        extension.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>SponsorBlock</strong> — Auto-skips sponsored segments in YouTube videos. Community-powered
        and surprisingly accurate. PewDiePie recommends this one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>ClearURLs</strong> — Strips tracking parameters from URLs. When you click a link from
        Google search results, there's usually a bunch of tracking garbage appended to the URL. This
        removes it.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Don't go overboard with extensions. Each one you add increases your browser's fingerprint
      (ironically making you more trackable in some ways). The core three — uBlock Origin, Unhook,
      and SponsorBlock — are enough for most people.
    </p>
    <label class="step-progress">
      <input type="checkbox" class="step-progress-checkbox" data-step-checkbox data-step-index="6" />
      <span class="step-progress-switch">
        <svg class="step-progress-check" width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 14 14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M3 7l3 3 5-5" /></svg>
      </span>
      <span class="step-progress-label">Mark step as done</span>
    </label>
  </div>

  <div class="step"="7">
    <span class="step-number">Step 7</span>
    <h3>Set Your New Browser as Default</h3>
    <p>
      Make your new browser the system default so every link you click opens in it instead of Chrome.
      On Windows: <strong>Settings &rarr; Apps &rarr; Default Apps &rarr; Web browser</strong>.
      On macOS: <strong>System Preferences &rarr; General &rarr; Default web browser</strong>.
      On Linux, your desktop environment's settings will have this option.
    </p>
    <p>
      After a week of using your new browser without issues, uninstall Chrome. If you need a Chromium-based
      browser as a fallback for that one weird site, keep Brave around for that purpose.
    </p>
    <label class="step-progress">
      <input type="checkbox" class="step-progress-checkbox" data-step-checkbox data-step-index="7" />
      <span class="step-progress-switch">
        <svg class="step-progress-check" width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 14 14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M3 7l3 3 5-5" /></svg>
      </span>
      <span class="step-progress-label">Mark step as done</span>
    </label>
  </div>

  <div class="flowing-line"></div>

  <h2>What You've Gained</h2>
  <div class="content-block">
    <p>In about five minutes of work, you've accomplished:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Stopped feeding Google your entire browsing history</li>
      <li>Blocked ads and trackers on every website you visit</li>
      <li>Made your YouTube experience intentional instead of algorithmic</li>
      <li>Started using a search engine that doesn't profile you</li>
      <li>Protected yourself from Manifest V3 killing your ad blocker</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      This is the foundation. Everything else on this site builds on top of this. You went from "ignorant"
      to "savior of your own browsing experience" in the time it takes to make a cup of coffee.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="flowing-line"></div>

  <DownsidesSection
    intro="We're not going to pretend this is all sunshine. Here's what might annoy you:"
    items={[
      {
        title: "Some websites break with strict privacy settings",
        description: "Banking sites, government portals, and some e-commerce sites may not work properly. You'll occasionally need to disable uBlock Origin for a specific site (click the extension icon → click the power button to whitelist)."
      },
      {
        title: "Chrome extensions don't all work in Firefox",
        description: "If you're switching to Firefox or LibreWolf, some Chrome-only extensions won't be available. Most popular ones have Firefox versions, but niche ones might not. Brave avoids this since it's Chromium-based."
      },
      {
        title: "DuckDuckGo results aren't always as good as Google's",
        description: "For most searches, they're fine. For very specific, localized, or time-sensitive queries, Google is still better. Use the <strong>!g</strong> bang when you need to."
      },
      {
        title: "You lose Google's password sync ecosystem",
        description: "If you relied on Chrome's built-in password manager, you'll need an alternative. We recommend <a href='/guides/self-hosting'>Vaultwarden</a> (covered in the self-hosting guide) or <a href='https://bitwarden.com/' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Bitwarden's</a> free cloud tier."
      },
      {
        title: "YouTube without recommendations feels empty at first",
        description: "You'll open YouTube and see... nothing. That's the point. It takes a few days to adjust to actually deciding what you want to watch. Stick with it."
      },
    ]}
  />

  <DeepDiveLinks links={[
    { label: "Private Web Browsers", url: "https://www.privacyguides.org/en/desktop-browsers/" },
    { label: "Search Engines", url: "https://www.privacyguides.org/en/search-engines/" },
    { label: "Browser Extensions", url: "https://www.privacyguides.org/en/browser-extensions/" },
  ]} />

</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .browser-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-4);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .browser-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }

  .browser-card {
    padding: var(--space-5);
    background: rgba(26, 29, 35, 0.5);
    backdrop-filter: blur(4px);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-muted);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .browser-card-bg {
    position: absolute;
    right: -10px;
    bottom: -10px;
    width: 120px;
    height: 120px;
    opacity: 0.08;
    pointer-events: none;
    user-select: none;
    object-fit: contain;
  }

  .browser-card h3 {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    margin-top: 0;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }

  .browser-card p {
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: var(--leading-relaxed);
  }

  .browser-card a {
    color: var(--text-accent);
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .browser-card a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
</style>
